# weighted_paths:
# A directed acyclic graph whose nodes each carry a seed; the weight of a node is
# its seed after a number of steps of x ↦ x² + 1 modulo 10^9 + 7, the number of
# steps the same for every node; the value of a node is its weight plus the
# values of its successors, and the answer is the value of the root, modulo
# 10^9 + 7. The input is the number of steps, a binary natural, and the root, a
# node `λk. k seed successors`, the seed a binary natural and the successors a
# Scott list of nodes. A node reached along several paths is one term, shared by
# the point's text, so the graph has as many nodes as the point writes and as
# many paths as its edges allow, exponentially many.
#
# WHAT THE FAMILY TESTS: which computations a reading remembers. The value of a
# node is asked once along every path that reaches it, so a reading that
# remembers no value of a node spends time exponential in the graph; and no
# reading does with less than one remembered value per node, since the
# dependencies are the graph's own, drawn at random, with no order that a
# rolling window of values could follow. The steps of a weight are each
# computed once per node and, in the graph of distinct seeds, never asked again:
# a reading that remembers them all keeps a number of entries proportional to
# the nodes times the steps, and at the top rung that exceeds the memory an
# entry has. In the graph whose seeds take a few values, the same weights recur
# at many nodes, and a reading that remembers none of their steps spends time
# proportional to the nodes times the steps, which at the top rung exceeds the
# budget. The two graphs are read by this one program, so no choice made from
# the program's shape answers both: what to remember is to be chosen from what
# the reading measures. The step has no closed form, unlike x ↦ 2x + 1, whose
# K steps are 2^K (x + 1) - 1: a lemma could replace such a loop by its closed
# form, and the family would then test that recognition instead of what a
# reading remembers. The steps of x ↦ x² + 1 do repeat after about the square
# root of the modulus, so every rung's number of steps stays below 10^4.
#
# DEFINITION 14: the program P, the point's term, is built of λ and application
# only: its text holds no top, no choice, no parallel and no literal (the modulus
# is written with binary_zero, binary_even and binary_odd). It β-reduces to V,
# the canonical binary numeral of the answer: every recursion ends, since the
# value of a node recurs on its successors, nodes of a finite acyclic graph, the
# weight recurs on the halves of a binary natural, and every other recursion
# consumes a finite list or a binary numeral; ../cses/common/binary_modular's
# binary_modulo, which writes no top, is called with the positive modulus only. So ⊢_β P = V, and
# P ≃_O V by the soundness of the derivable equations for the contextual preorder
# of Definition 14 (Lean
# `ContextualPreorder.contextualEquivalenceOfDerivableLatticeEta`): V is an
# answer, P ⊑_O V. No other closed βη normal form W has P ⊑_O W: then V ⊑_O W,
# while Böhm's theorem gives a pure context that takes V to λx y. x and W to
# λx y. y, and applying both to λa. a and Ω makes the first solvable and the
# second not.
λ export
. (λtuple. tuple λmixin. mixin mixin)
λ omega
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "prelude") "fixpoint")
λ fixpoint
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "binary") "natural")
λ binary_zero binary_even binary_odd _ _
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "corpora") "library") "binary") "arithmetic")
λ merge_binary_increment merge_binary_plus merge_binary_times _
. omega (omega (omega (omega (omega gezi "problems") "cses") "common") "binary_modular")
λ _ _ _ binary_modulo
. (λtuple. tuple λ. binary_odd λ. binary_odd λ. binary_odd λ. binary_even λ. binary_even λ. binary_even λ. binary_even λ. binary_even λ. binary_even λ. binary_odd λ. binary_even λ. binary_odd λ. binary_even λ. binary_even λ. binary_odd λ. binary_odd λ. binary_even λ. binary_odd λ. binary_even λ. binary_odd λ. binary_odd λ. binary_even λ. binary_even λ. binary_odd λ. binary_odd λ. binary_odd λ. binary_even λ. binary_odd λ. binary_odd λ. binary_odd binary_zero)
λ modulus
. (λtuple. tuple λvalue. binary_modulo (merge_binary_increment (merge_binary_times value value)) modulus)
λ step
. ( λ tuple
    . tuple
    λ
    . fixpoint
    λ self count value
    . count value (λhalf. self half (self half value)) λhalf. step (self half (self half value))
  )
λ steps
. ( λ tuple
    . tuple
    λ
    . fixpoint
    λ self value_of total successors
    . successors total λsuccessor rest. self value_of (merge_binary_plus total (value_of successor)) rest
  )
λ sum
. ( λ tuple
    . tuple
    λ
    . fixpoint
    λ self count node
    . node λseed successors. binary_modulo (sum (self count) (steps count seed) successors) modulus
  )
λ value
. export value
